*.csv
*.xlsx
*.h5

__pycache__/

# Model checkpoints live under models/; copies kept next to the code that
# loads them are duplicates (xai_test/SpCas9/'s copy is byte-identical to
# models/Multi-Cas_1IN_9Hydra_Divide_Testset.keras).
xai_test/SpCas9/*.keras

# 9-Hydra v2 training output: the smoke run's throwaway artifacts, and every
# checkpoint until there is a decision to keep one (it would go under models/,
# like every other checkpoint). The full run's `training_log.json` stays
# tracked - small, and the reproducibility record the checkpoint is traced by
# (#47).
xai_test/SpCas9/v2_train/**/*.keras
xai_test/SpCas9/v2_train/smoke/

# The Cas12a training data (Kim HK et al., "Deep learning improves prediction of
# CRISPR-Cpf1 guide RNA activity", Nat Biotechnol 36:239-241, 2018 -
# supplementary table; the raw sheet's 15,002 HEK293T-measured 34 bp targets
# become 15,000 usable rows once the two with no target are dropped). 2MB, already
# covered by `*.xlsx` above; spelled out because it is required to train and is
# deliberately absent from the repo. The project's shared copy lives in Google
# Drive under `project_shared/data/`.
cas12a/dataset/*.xlsx

# Test fixtures are the exception to the `*.csv` rule at the top of this file:
# they are small, synthetic, and `cas12a/test/` cannot run without them.
!cas12a/test/fixtures/*.csv

# Cas12a 5-fold output (#92). The checkpoint is ignored and its metrics JSON is
# tracked, the same split 9-Hydra's `training_log.json` uses above: the JSON is
# small and is the only source of this model's performance numbers, while the
# weights are large and matter only once something decides to adopt them (at
# which point they go under `models/`, like every other adopted checkpoint).
cas12a/artifacts/*.keras

# NT-Cas12a run output under `cas12a/model/result/<run_id>/` (#105). Same split
# as the two rules above: the evidence JSON is tracked because it is the only
# source of this model's performance numbers and its reproducibility record,
# while the weights are large and matter only once something decides to adopt
# them (at which point they go under `models/`, like every other adopted
# checkpoint). The authoritative copy of a whole run is the durable export in
# Google Drive under `cas12a-nt/runs/`, and every file in it is recoverable by
# the sha256 recorded in that run's `durable_export.json`.
#
# `selected_export/` is the 1.8GiB safetensors model plus its tokenizer.
cas12a/model/result/**/selected_export/
# The Drive download, byte-identical to the directory it was extracted into.
cas12a/model/result/*.zip
# Bulk per-row inputs, reproducible from the source sheet and the recorded
# seed-42 split: tokenized sequences (13MB) and the split's row indices (1.6MB).
# Their sha256 stays in `configuration.json`, which is tracked.
cas12a/model/result/**/data.json
cas12a/model/result/**/partition.json

# 9-Hydra v3 run output under `xai_test/SpCas9_NT_analysis/result/` (#129). Same
# split as the three rules above: the metrics CSV, `config.json`, `mu_sd.npy` and
# the tokenizer files are tracked because they are the only local source of this
# model's performance numbers and the only way to reproduce its tokenisation and
# label scale without the checkpoint, while the weights are large and matter only
# once something decides to adopt them (at which point they go under `models/`,
# like every other adopted checkpoint). The authoritative copy lives in Google
# Drive under `SpCas9/spcas9_v3/nt_v3_seed42`, which is also what the Colab
# drivers read; nothing local needs the weights.
#
# 1.8GiB safetensors checkpoint.
xai_test/SpCas9_NT_analysis/result/model.safetensors
# The run's metrics summary is the exception to the `*.csv` rule at the top of
# this file: it is a single 212-byte row, not bulk per-row data, and it is the
# only local source of v3's performance numbers.
!xai_test/SpCas9_NT_analysis/result/nt_v3_seed42_results.csv

# 9-Hydra v3 XAI artifacts (#129). `predictions.csv` is bulk per-row output
# (5,186 rows, 1.1MB) and stays under the `*.csv` rule at the top of this file:
# it is exactly reproducible from the pinned checkpoint and the pinned split by
# `v3_predict.export_predictions`, and its sha256 is recorded in the tracked
# `predictions_checksum.json` beside it. Same for the bulk `.npz` arrays the
# ISM, IG and rollout runs will write; their sidecars carry the digests.
xai_test/SpCas9_NT_analysis/artifacts/**/*.npz
